title: Content Hierarchy
files:
  - em.prog:em.examples.basic/Ex02_BlinkerP.em.ts
  - .assets/img-100-02-01.png
  - .assets/img-100-02-02.png

steps:
  - cmds:
      - open 1
    focus: [1, 1]
    acts: ["$reveal"]
    text: |

      {[ht,$start]&thinsp;EM&bull;Script Content Hierarchy}

      This tour has two objectives which revolve around the **EM&bull;Builder Content** view seen in the **Primary Sidebar**:

      {[bi,arrow_circle_right]} Understanding the elements comprising this view.<br>
      {[bi,arrow_circle_right]} Showing how to add *new* elements to the hierarchy.

      As a live example, we'll return to the {[cu]BlinkerP} program introduced in the previous tour {[tr,100/01/04]}.

      Click the {[cb,target]} button to reveal this program's **Package &middot; Bucket &middot; Unit** elements within the content hierarchy.

      The **Next** stops on this tour will provide more infor&shy;mation about these **EM&bull;Script** [**EMS**] elements.

  - cmds:
      - open 1
      - mark 1 A 2
    focus: [1, 1]
    acts: ["$reveal"]
    text: |

      {[ht,account_tree]&thinsp;EM&bull;Script Unit &ndash; Source File}

      The program informally named {[cu]BlinkerP} actually re&shy;sides in a **Unit** source file named {[cx]Ex02_BlinkerP.em.ts}.

      The statement labeled {[bm,A]} declares the _flavor_ of this particular **Unit** &ndash; here, of an **EM&bull;Script Module**.

      Upcoming tours will visit encounter other **Unit** flavors, as we dive deeper into the **EMS** language.

      <div class="em-info">

      You'll also learn why all **EMS Unit** source files end with a {[cx].ts} extension.&thinsp;  [Can you guess why&thinsp;??]

      </div>

      Let's move on and talk about the **EMS Bucket** associ&shy;ated with our {[cu]Blinker} program.

  - cmds:
      - open 1
    focus: [1, 1]
    acts: ["$reveal"]
    text: |

      {[ht,account_tree]&thinsp;EM&bull;Script Bucket &ndash; Namespace}

      The **Unit** corresponding to our {[cu]BlinkerP} program lives inside an **EMS Bucket** named {[cx]em.examples.basic}

      Logically a namespace (and physically a folder), a **Unit** of the *same* name can reside in more than one **Bucket**.

      As a rule, each **EMS Bucket** has a *globally unique* name comprising multiple identifiers joined by dots.

      In fact, its first identifier alone defines a unique scope:

      {[bi,arrow_circle_right]} <code>em.&ast;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</code>&mdash; **EM Foundation** content<br>
      {[bi,arrow_circle_right]} <code>nordic.&ast;&nbsp;</code>&mdash; **Nordic Semiconductor** content<br>
      {[bi,arrow_circle_right]} <code>rpi.&ast;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</code>&mdash; **Raspberry Pi** content<br>

      As you'll now see, the _same_ set of prefixes also ensure the uniqueness of **EMS Package** names.

  - cmds:
      - open 1
      - mark 1 B 5
    focus: [1, 1]
    acts: ["$reveal"]
    text: |

      {[ht,account_tree]&thinsp;EM&bull;Script Package &ndash; Container}

      The **Bucket &middot; Unit** elements named {[cx]em.examples.basic/} {[cx]Ex02_BlinkerP.em.ts} live inside the  {[cx]em.prog} **Package**.

      Seemingly redundant, an **EMS Package** serves as a uniquely-named *container* for **Bucket &middot; Unit** *content*.

      A prefixed naming scheme enables us to identify the content *supplier* independent of the content itself.

      Line {[bm,B]} shows how one **Unit** can import another using only the latter's fully-qualified `bucket/Unit` name.

      <div class="em-info">

      In fact, **EMS Package** names will rarely appear within **EMS Unit** source code.

      </div>

  - cmds:
      - view 2
    text: |

      {[ht,snippet_folder]&thinsp;Files and Folders}

      We've already mentioned how an **EMS Unit** corres&shy;ponds to a source file of the same name.

      This pattern continues with each **EMS Package &middot; Bucket** as well &ndash; mapping to similarly named folders.

      To switch perspective, right-click {[dc,1]} on *any* visible icon within the **Content** view and select **Reveal in Explorer** {[dc,2]}.

      <div class="em-info">

      You'll now see the same element as a file or folder with&shy;in your local **Git** clone of the `emporium` repository.

      </div>

      To return to its **Content** view, click the **EM&bull;Builder** icon {[dc,3]} at the top of the **Activity Bar**.

  - cmds:
      - view 3
    text: |

      {[ht,note_add]&thinsp;Adding EM&bull;Script Content}

      {[DC,1]} Right-click on the **Content** view's **EM&bull;Script Source** item and select its **New Package** command.

      {[DC,2]} When prompted, give the new **Package** a simple  yet unique name of the form <code>zgit.&lt;username&gt;</code>.

      {[DC,3]} Now, right-click on the new **Package** once it appears and select its **New Bucket** command.

      {[DC,4]} When prompted, give the new **Bucket** a unique name such as <code>zgit.&lt;username&gt;.mystuff</code>.

      {[DC,5]}Finally, select **New Program** after right-clicking the new **Bucket** &ndash; and name your program `Blinky`.

      To *delete* a **Package &middot; Bucket &middot; Unit** from the content hierarchy, select **Remove** from its right-click menu.

  - cmds:
    text: |

      {[ht,$steps]&thinsp;Next Steps}

      Well done &ndash; you're now officially dangerous.🔥

      Feel free to scavenge the {[cx]em.examples.basic} **Bucket** for more **EMS** code you can copy&thinsp;&middot;&thinsp;edit&thinsp;&middot;&thinsp;build.

      While covered by the **Basic Examples** tours, why not get familiar with this "starter-ware" right now&thinsp;??

      To explore an example, first open its `.em.ts` file and then click the **Build&thinsp;&middot;&thinsp;Load** button in the **Content** view.

      And don't forget about that **Discard changes** command we highlighted on the previous tour-stop&thinsp;!!

      {[hc]}
